{
  "git": {
    "rules": [
      {
        "args": [
          "commit",
          "--amend"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "Agent never amends — the commit is already made, so commit forward: put the fix in its own commit. Amending rewrites HEAD, and whatever the old commit held that is not staged now leaves only a reflog entry behind. On an unpushed branch a second commit costs nothing (squash at merge time); on a pushed one, rewriting is the user's call."
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "push",
          "--force"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "Never force push"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "push",
          "--force-with-lease"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "Never force push"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "*",
          "--no-verify"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "Never skip hooks"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "filter-branch"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "filter-branch rewrites history"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "filter-repo"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "filter-repo rewrites history"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "reset"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "Don't reset history. Commit forward (new commit / `git revert`) or ask the user to run `git reset` themselves."
      },
      {
        "args_glob": "clean*-f*",
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "`git clean -f` deletes untracked files with no undo — same class as `rm` outside the scratch dir. Preview instead: `git clean -n`. Your own throwaway files belong in `.scratch/`, where `rm` is allowed. If real untracked files must go, leave the deletion to the user so it stays visible."
      },
      {
        "fn": "git_discards_worktree_changes",
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "This overwrites working-tree files with a committed version, and whatever it overwrites was never committed or stashed — no reflog entry, no dangling object, nothing to recover from. It is the most-reported way an agent destroys a user's uncommitted work, and the tree you are about to revert holds their edits as well as yours. To unstage without touching the file: `git restore --staged <path>`. To undo an edit you made, edit it back with the Edit tool, which shows a diff. To see what would be lost: `git diff -- <path>`. Reverting the user's working tree is their call — leave the command to them."
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "stash",
          "drop"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "`git stash drop` throws away a stash entry with no undo, and the stack is shared with the user — the entry you drop may be theirs. The reported pattern is exactly this: a `stash pop` hits a conflict, the stash gets dropped to clean up, and days of work are gone. Inspect instead: `git stash list`, then `git stash show -p 'stash@{0}'`. If a pop conflicted, resolve the conflict — the entry stays until it applies cleanly, which is the point. Dropping is the user's call."
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "stash",
          "clear"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "`git stash clear` deletes every stash entry in the repo at once, including ones the user pushed before you started, with no undo. There is no agent task that needs this. Read the stack instead: `git stash list`. If a specific entry is genuinely in your way, leave the removal to the user."
      },
      {
        "fn": "git_branch_force_delete",
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": "You're force-deleting a branch that was never pushed, so its commits live only in this clone. They stay in the reflog for now — `git reflog` finds the tip and `git branch <name> <sha>` restores it — but nothing else does. If the work matters, push the branch before deleting."
      },
      {
        "fn": "git_branch_off_protected",
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": "You're branching off something other than main/master, not the trunk. Double-check this is what the user asked for — if you meant to branch off the trunk, do `git switch main && git switch -c <name>`, or name the base explicitly: `git switch -c <name> main`. Rooting branches at the trunk keeps PR diffs clean and avoids accidental stacking."
      },
      {
        "fn": "git_branch_off_stale_main",
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": "Your local main/master differs from origin (last fetched) — you're branching off a stale base, so the new branch may miss recent commits. If you wanted a fresh base, you probably meant to pull first: `git switch main && git pull --ff-only`, then create the branch. (Compares the cached origin ref; fires only on a provable divergence.)"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "rebase"
        ],
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "Agent never rebases — commit forward or ask the user to rebase manually"
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "cherry-pick"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "merge"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "revert"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "fn": "git_write_on_merged_branch",
        "decision": "deny",
        "reason": "This branch's PR is already merged, so the branch is finished — a merged PR never picks up new commits, and anything committed here lands nowhere. INSTEAD: start the follow-up work on a fresh branch off updated trunk — `git checkout main && git pull && git checkout -b <feature>` — and `git cherry-pick <sha>` anything you already committed on this branch."
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "commit"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "fn": "git_config_read",
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "config"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": "You're writing git config, not reading it. It's reversible (`git config --unset <key>`), but check the scope you meant: no flag writes this repo's `.git/config`, `--global` changes every repo on the machine."
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "status"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "log"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
      },
      {
        "args": [
          "diff"
        ],
        "decision": "allow",
        "reason": ""
